{"context": "The author of an AI-safety research organization is reflecting on the organization's future direction as it considers restructuring. They have surveyed a landscape of safety-adjacent areas — automated evaluation infrastructure, frontier agent observability and control, mechanistic interpretability, red-teaming, and AI-relevant computer security — and noted several existing organizations, both non-profit and for-profit, working in each. They must decide whether the organization should remain purely research-focused or also build commercial products.\n\nQuestion: Should the organization move beyond pure safety research into building commercial safety products, and if so, under what conditions?", "options": ["Build commercial monitoring and control products for AI coding agents while continuing the organization's established core safety research work in parallel.", "Build commercial products only in areas where revenue directly scales safety coverage, keeping the rest of the organization research-focused and staffed independently of revenue."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
